      "pd.mykarage.answer": "My Karage is a customer iOS app for car owners in Saudi Arabia: maintenance history, invoices, a garage network, offers, booking and payment. I owned the app end to end in Swift and SwiftUI, shipped it in 2022 and maintained it through 2026, as it grew to 5,000+ users.",
      "pd.mykarage.fpart": "The iOS app",
      "pd.mykarage.fwhen": "2022 to 2026",
      "pd.mykarage.fusers": "5,000+",
      "pd.mykarage.n.role": "The ASP.NET backend was built by the backend team. The app talks to it; it is not mine.",
      "pd.karagekash.answer": "Karage Kash is an iPadOS point of sale for car-service stores: the cashier rings up services, builds the cart and issues the invoice from one screen. Version one existed; I led version two, rebuilt in about three months and shipped in 2025. It is in production use.",
      "pd.karagekash.fpart": "Version two",
      "pd.karagekash.fbuild": "About three months",
      "pd.karagekash.n.role": "Version one existed before me. Epson printing was integrated with a teammate, and another developer joined later on smaller features.",
      "pd.karagekash.n.hl": "Keep the whole flow on a single screen, so the cashier never loses context mid-sale.",
      "pd.jamaatna.answer": "Jamaatna is my own bilingual platform for weddings and events: digital invitations, one-tap RSVP, QR entry codes and a live attendance dashboard, in Arabic and English. I do all of it, from product design and code to localisation and marketing. It launched in 2026.",
      "pd.jamaatna.fpart": "All of it, my own product",
      "pd.jamaatna.n.hl": "A scan at the door updates the attendance dashboard for everyone watching, with no refresh.",
      "pd.myvenue.answer": "MyVenue is an MVP marketplace for booking event venues in Riyadh: halls, chalets, farms and majlis on a map, with ratings, favourites and bookings. Arabic first, with English. I built the frontend and backend on Replit, as a freelance build for Howamesh.",
      "pd.myvenue.fpart": "Frontend and backend",
      "pd.myvenue.fstatus": "MVP",
      "pd.myvenue.n.hl": "Search starts from the map, in an Arabic-first layout, the way people here look for a venue.",
      "pd.dashboard.answer": "A Next.js and TypeScript dashboard for a car-services business, used by owners and garages. I owned the invoicing inside it: ZATCA Phase 2 tax invoices in simplified and A4 formats, and quotations that flow into invoices. The rest of the dashboard I supported. It is a private company project.",
      "pd.dashboard.fpart": "The invoicing UI",
      "pd.dashboard.fwhen": "2025 to 2026",
      "pd.dashboard.fstatus": "Private, in use",
      "pd.dashboard.n.role": "Reporting, analytics, customers, inventory and staff were the wider team’s. I supported them; I did not own them.",
      "pd.dashboard.n.hl": "Invoices must meet ZATCA Phase 2, the Saudi e-invoicing standard, in both formats owners rely on daily.",
      "pd.4service.answer": "4service.co is a bilingual marketing site for a car-service center in Saudi Arabia: maintenance, inspection and detailing services, a gallery, customer reviews and one-tap WhatsApp contact. A freelance project I designed and built from scratch in vanilla HTML, CSS and JavaScript, shipped in 2025.",
      "pd.4service.fpart": "Design and build",
      "pd.4service.n.hl": "No framework: vanilla HTML, CSS and JS, bilingual with right-to-left, and WhatsApp one tap away.",
      "pd.saleh.answer": "A bilingual personal-brand site for Salih Bawarith, a Riyadh F&B operations manager, specialty-coffee expert and Arabic calligrapher. His career reads as a coffee “roast curve”, with galleries for his calligraphy and events. I designed and built it in vanilla HTML, CSS and JavaScript; it shipped in 2026.",
      "pd.saleh.fpart": "Concept, design and build",
      "pd.saleh.n.role": "All content lives in one JavaScript data file, so Salih updates his timeline and galleries without touching HTML.",
      "pd.saleh.n.hl": "Braided lanes keep the roles he ran at the same time legible, instead of flattening them into one column.",
      "pd.goldprice.answer": "Metals Prices is a Raycast extension for precious-metal prices per gram: gold, silver, platinum and palladium, with purity selection, historical averages and a choice of display currency. It started as Gold Price. I designed and built it in TypeScript and published it on the Raycast Store.",
      "pd.goldprice.fpart": "All of it, my own",
      "pd.goldprice.fstatus": "On the Raycast Store",
      "pd.goldprice.n.hl": "Show what a request costs before loading an average.",
      "pd.goldprice.n.role": "It has to stay within the free metals.dev API tier, so results are cached.",
      "pd.turathiyat.answer": "Turathiyat is a Riyadh dealer in rare banknotes, coins and stamps whose business lived inside a classifieds marketplace. I built the whole system: a scraper, an Arabic catalogue on the shop’s own domain, a phone-first owner panel and a marketplace sync. Every buy button starts a WhatsApp message with the piece’s reference code.",
      "pd.turathiyat.fpart": "All of it",
      "pd.turathiyat.fpages": "About 130",
      "pd.turathiyat.fpieces": "119, 315 photos",
      "pd.turathiyat.n.overview": "Codes run from TRH-0001, are assigned once and never reused, because they end up in real conversations.",
      "pd.turathiyat.n.role": "The marketplace is still where the inventory starts. The sync reads from it, and the owner approves every change.",
      "pd.turathiyat.n.features": "Nothing reaches the live site unreviewed. Only a sold-state flip applies on its own.",
      "pd.turathiyat.n.ux": "A sold piece keeps its page, its URL and its rankings. The state flips and the buy button disappears.",
      "pd.turathiyat.n.hl": "WhatsApp lets a sender edit the prefilled text. Two records to reconcile, not one to trust.",
      "pd.kidsstory.answer": "Marwah Stories Library turns an author’s page-by-page scripts into illustrated Arabic books a child can leaf through. I built everything between the script and the book: a locked illustration style, AI artwork made against it, Python page tooling and a right-to-left flip-book reader. Two books, 64 pages, are live.",
      "pd.kidsstory.fpart": "Pipeline, tooling, reader",
      "pd.kidsstory.fstatus": "Live, two books",
      "pd.kidsstory.fpages": "64 illustrated",
      "pd.kidsstory.n.role": "The stories are Marwah Alhalabi’s. The artwork is AI-generated against my style profile, with a human eye on every page.",
      "pd.kidsstory.n.hl": "The slicer’s grid detection assumes panels sitting on a white sheet.",
      "pd.howamesh.answer": "Howamesh is a Saudi creative collective that had a brand deck and no website. I built a bilingual landing page for its principles, its fields of work, an archive of 39 delivered works and a contact form: a static site with no framework, where each language is a real page. The brand itself is the collective’s own.",
      "pd.howamesh.fpart": "Everything but the brand",
      "pd.howamesh.n.role": "The palette, the illustrations and the wordmark are the collective’s own. The page transcribes their deck; it does not reinvent it.",
      "pd.howamesh.n.type": "Arabic body copy is set one weight heavier than the Latin, so the thin joins and the dots survive.",
      "pd.howamesh.n.hl": "Render Arabic and look at it after every font change. A correct byte count proves nothing about shaping.",
      "pd.alnajim.answer": "Alnajim is a bilingual landing page for Abdulrahman Alnajim, a Saudi filmmaker who shoots, edits and colour-grades. It turns his published films into enquiries: a visitor picks one of four services and sends a request that already says what they want. Arabic is the default; both languages are real prerendered pages.",
      "pd.alnajim.fpart": "Everything but the identity",
      "pd.alnajim.ffilms": "16 published",
      "pd.alnajim.n.role": "The palette, lockup, badge and ornamental border come from his brand guideline, extracted as true vector rather than redrawn.",
      "pd.alnajim.n.features": "The four services work as a filter, and the choice carries into the request form.",
      "pd.alnajim.n.hl": "A seeded random generator, so every load draws exactly the same thing.",
